<h1>GitLab for the model team</h1>
<p><em>How a government model team runs a SysML v1 &rarr; v2 port, and the model's maintenance afterwards, in GitLab:
the features, why each one is worth the trouble, the scripts that set them up, and filled-in samples of every text
field.</em></p>

<h2 id="who">Who this is for</h2>
<p>This guide is for systems engineers who have done configuration management for years, with documents, spreadsheets,
briefings and, lately, a Cameo model, and who have now been told to "do DevSecOps". If that model turned into a spider
web that nobody could review, and the new direction feels like one more tool on top, this guide is for you.</p>
<p>Three things to know before you start:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Most of DevSecOps is configuration management you already know</strong>, done in smaller pieces and
      checked by machine. A merge request is a change request. An approval is a CCB signature. The pipeline is a
      configuration audit. A protected tag is a baseline. The <a href="#glossary">glossary</a> translates every term.</li>
  <li><strong>Each section below starts with "Why bother"</strong>: the objection a seasoned engineer raises (because
      the old way worked, or seemed to), and what the feature gives back for the effort.</li>
  <li><strong>Every new word is defined the first time it appears</strong>, and again in the glossary.</li>
</ul>
<div class="why"><b>Why bother at all?</b> The documents-and-briefings way scaled with people: every review, audit and
status chart was somebody's week. The Cameo model added a tool without changing that, and grew into a web nobody could
review. Here the model is plain text, split into owned pieces; every change is small, shown line by line, reviewed,
and audited by machine before it reaches the master copy; and status comes out of the work itself. The effort moves
from chasing to doing.</div>

<div class="note">The running example is <strong>Tidewater</strong>, a fictional coastal weather-observation network.
The prime contractor (the fictional Northwind Marine Systems) keeps the authoritative SysML v1 model in Cameo and the
requirements in DOORS, and delivers them as <em>drops</em>. The government team ports each drop to SysML v2 text in a GitLab
project and keeps it current: Jim, the MBSE lead responsible for the model, directed this year to follow DevSecOps for
the first time; Bob, the MBSE engineer; and Tom, the configuration management engineer, who also runs the pipeline.
None of them had done DevSecOps before. Every name and value is notional. The simulation of 18 months of that work is in
<code>sim/tidewater-sim.pl</code>; the walk-through of its first 12 weeks is <a href="TIDEWATER.html">TIDEWATER.html</a>.</div>

