<h2 id="why">What GitLab does, in terms you know</h2>
<p>GitLab is a web server that holds the model's files under version control (a <em>repository</em>: every file, with
every change ever made to it) and the work around them: change requests, reviews, audits, action items, baselines.
Each feature below carries one job you already do. The table is the map; the sections after it are the detail.
GitLab is sold in tiers: <em>Free</em>, <em>Premium</em> and <em>Ultimate</em>; the last column says which one a
feature needs.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>You have always needed</th><th>GitLab feature</th><th>How Tidewater uses it</th><th>Tier</th></tr>
<tr><td>One place for the model, its history and who changed what</td><td>Project (Git repository)</td><td><code>tidewater/model</code>; SysML v2 text under <code>model/</code></td><td>Free</td></tr>
<tr><td>Released baselines that never change</td><td>Protected branch <code>main</code>, protected tags <code>baseline-*</code>, Releases</td><td>A baseline is a merge to <code>main</code>, a tag and a release with notes</td><td>Free</td></tr>
<tr><td>Integration without breaking the model</td><td>Protected branch <code>develop</code>, merge requests, pipelines must succeed</td><td>Every change arrives by MR; the gate is lint 0/0</td><td>Free</td></tr>
<tr><td>The prime's changes merged with ours</td><td>A protected vendor branch and Git's three-way merge</td><td><code>vendor/prime</code> holds raw converter output of each drop</td><td>Free</td></tr>
<tr><td>The right person reviews each part</td><td><code>CODEOWNERS</code>, approval rules</td><td>Bob owns structure, Jim owns requirements, Tom owns the pipeline</td><td>Premium (enforced); Free shows owners</td></tr>
<tr><td>Checks nobody can skip</td><td>CI/CD pipelines, merge checks</td><td>grammar, lint, outline, secrets, trace, docs, delta, baseline</td><td>Free</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sprints</td><td>Milestones (Free) or iterations (Premium)</td><td><code>Sprint 1</code> &hellip; <code>Sprint 39</code>, two weeks each</td><td>Free</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stories and tasks</td><td>Issues and tasks, templates, quick actions</td><td>Story and task issues; "Part of #N"</td><td>Free</td></tr>
<tr><td>Epics and tomes</td><td>Group epics, multi-level epics (Premium/Ultimate) or labels (Free)</td><td><code>epic::*</code> and <code>tome::*</code> labels</td><td>Free (labels)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Work visible at a glance</td><td>Issue boards with label lists</td><td>The <em>Sprint</em> and <em>Prime</em> boards</td><td>Free</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mutually exclusive states</td><td>Scoped labels (<code>workflow::ready</code>)</td><td>One workflow, type and priority per issue</td><td>Premium (Free: the names still work, not exclusive)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Procedures and decisions</td><td>Wiki</td><td>Runbooks, Definitions of Ready and Done, decision records</td><td>Free</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weekly status</td><td>Pipeline schedules, the REST API</td><td><code>gitlab.pl export</code> feeds <code>bin/status-metrics.pl</code></td><td>Free</td></tr>
</table>

<h2 id="tools">Which GitLab DevSecOps tools this team uses, and why</h2>
<div class="why"><b>The objection:</b> "GitLab has hundreds of features. We are three people with a model to port; we
cannot learn all of that." <b>Why bother:</b> you should not learn all of it. The table below is the short list worth
the set-up time for a three-person model team, each with the job it takes off your hands. Everything else is
deliberately left off, and the second table says why, so nobody feels they are missing something.</div>
<p>Tiers are as of GitLab 17. Check your instance's edition (Help &gt; Help shows it); where a feature needs a tier you do
not have, the "Set up" column gives the Free-tier way.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Tier</th><th>Value for a 3-person MBSE team</th><th>Set up</th></tr>
<tr><td>Issue boards</td><td>Free</td><td>The action-item tracker everybody sees, with no spreadsheet to update: moving a card changes its status</td><td><code>[labels]</code> in <code>project.conf</code>; Plan &gt; Issue boards, one list per <code>workflow::</code> label</td></tr>
<tr><td>Scoped labels (<code>key::value</code>)</td><td>Premium</td><td>An item has exactly one status, type and priority; no "in progress and done" cards</td><td>Name labels <code>key::value</code> (setup does). On Free, the names still work; remove the old one yourself</td></tr>
<tr><td>Milestones as sprints</td><td>Free</td><td>The two-week time box and its list of work, in one place</td><td><code>[milestones] sprints = 2026-10-05 39 2</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Burndown and burnup charts</td><td>Premium</td><td>The sprint's progress drawn for you on the milestone page</td><td>Nothing: they appear on each milestone. On Free, use the journal's sprint report</td></tr>
<tr><td>Time tracking (<code>/estimate</code>, <code>/spend</code>)</td><td>Free</td><td>Sizes tasks in days, keeping the "under a week" rule visible; actuals for the next estimate</td><td>Use the quick actions; templates include <code>/estimate</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>CODEOWNERS</code></td><td>Free (shown) / Premium (enforced)</td><td>The CCB, per package: the right person approves each part of the model</td><td>Commit <code>CODEOWNERS</code>; <code>codeowners:yes</code> under <code>[branches]</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Approval rules</td><td>Premium</td><td>A minimum number of approvals on every change, and no self-approval</td><td><code>[approvals] Model review = 1 jim bob</code>; Settings &gt; Merge requests: prevent approval by author</td></tr>
<tr><td>Protected branches and tags</td><td>Free</td><td>Nobody changes the master copy or a baseline except through an approved change</td><td><code>[branches]</code>, <code>[tags]</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Push rules</td><td>Premium</td><td>The server rejects commits with no issue reference, files that should never be committed (exports, <code>.mdzip</code>), and secrets</td><td>Settings &gt; Repository &gt; Push rules: commit message regex <code>#\d+</code>, file name deny list <code>\.(mdzip|exe)$</code>, prevent secrets</td></tr>
<tr><td>Signed commits</td><td>Free (verified badge) / Premium (reject unsigned)</td><td>Proof of who made each change, beyond the name typed into Git</td><td>Each person adds an SSH or GPG signing key; <code>git config commit.gpgsign true</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>MR widgets: test results (JUnit)</td><td>Free</td><td>The MR shows which audit check failed and why, without opening a log</td><td>The template's <code>artifacts:reports:junit</code> (from <code>ci-reports.pl</code>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>MR widgets: Code Quality</td><td>Free (widget) / Premium (in the diff)</td><td>Shows the errors, warnings and TODO lines this change added or removed</td><td><code>artifacts:reports:codequality</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>MR widgets: metrics</td><td>Premium</td><td>Shows how the numbers moved: TODO lines, lint, trace completeness</td><td><code>artifacts:reports:metrics</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Secret Detection</td><td>Free (scan) / Ultimate (MR widget, vulnerability report)</td><td>A token or password committed by mistake is caught before it spreads</td><td><code>include: template: Security/Secret-Detection.gitlab-ci.yml</code>, plus the local <code>secrets</code> job</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pages</td><td>Free</td><td>A web site of the model's current state for the program office and the prime, who will never open the repository</td><td>The <code>pages</code> job; Deploy &gt; Pages shows the address; set access control to project members</td></tr>
<tr><td>Environments</td><td>Free</td><td>Records which commit the published site shows, and when it changed</td><td><code>environment: model-site</code> on the <code>pages</code> job</td></tr>
<tr><td>Releases</td><td>Free</td><td>Each baseline with its notes and evidence, on one page that lists every baseline</td><td>The <code>publish-release</code> job on <code>baseline-*</code> tags</td></tr>
<tr><td>Package registry (generic)</td><td>Free</td><td>Each prime drop kept exactly as delivered, by version, beside the model it became: the CDRL record</td><td>The <code>drop-package</code> job; nothing to configure (the job token authenticates)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Scheduled pipelines</td><td>Free</td><td>The weekly status computed on Monday morning, with no one typing it up</td><td>Build &gt; Pipeline schedules, Mondays 06:00; variable <code>STATUS_TOKEN</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Audit events</td><td>Premium</td><td>Who changed a protection, a member or a token, and when: the CM office's access log</td><td>Secure &gt; Audit events; review monthly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Value stream analytics</td><td>Free (project) / Premium (custom stages)</td><td>How long work takes from issue to merge, so the team sees where it waits</td><td>Analyze &gt; Value stream analytics; nothing to set up</td></tr>
<tr><td>DORA metrics</td><td>Ultimate</td><td>Change frequency, lead time, failure rate and restore time, computed from the <code>model-site</code> environment</td><td>Analyze &gt; CI/CD analytics; mark <code>model-site</code> as a production-tier environment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wiki</td><td>Free</td><td>The process, the definitions and the decisions, versioned like the model</td><td><code>gitlab.pl wiki put</code> each page from <code>templates/wiki/</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Snippets</td><td>Free</td><td>Shared one-off commands and SysML v2 examples, without a file in the repository</td><td>Code &gt; Snippets; keep them project-level, not personal</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>What this team deliberately does not use yet</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Tier</th><th>Why not now</th><th>When to revisit</th></tr>
<tr><td>SAST, dependency scanning, container scanning</td><td>Free (SAST) / Ultimate</td><td>They scan application code, libraries and container images. The repository holds a model in text and no code, libraries or images; Secret Detection covers the one real risk</td><td>When the project starts shipping code (an analysis tool, a generator) or images</td></tr>
<tr><td>GitLab Requirements Management</td><td>Ultimate</td><td>DOORS stays the requirements authority under the contract. A second requirements database would drift from it; the trace job compares DOORS with the model instead</td><td>If the program moves requirements authority out of DOORS</td></tr>
<tr><td>Merge trains</td><td>Premium</td><td>They queue many concurrent merges so each is tested on top of the others. Three people rarely have two MRs ready at once, and the merged-results pipeline already tests on the latest target</td><td>When the team grows or merges start colliding</td></tr>
<tr><td>Epics and iterations</td><td>Premium</td><td>Useful, but labels and milestones do the same job on Free, and the process is written to work either way</td><td>When the instance has Premium: move epic text into group epics</td></tr>
<tr><td>Auto DevOps, Kubernetes, feature flags, container registry</td><td>Free and up</td><td>There is no application to build, deploy or switch on</td><td>Not for a model repository</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI assistants (GitLab Duo)</td><td>add-on</td><td>Needs its own approval for government data; see <code>docs/OLLAMA.md</code> for the kit's local, approved-model route</td><td>After an approval decision</td></tr>
</table>

<h2 id="hierarchy">The work hierarchy in GitLab</h2>
<div class="why"><b>The objection:</b> "We have a WBS and an IMS. Why tomes, epics, stories and tasks?" <b>Why bother:</b> they are a WBS, sized by time instead of by contract line, so you always know how far away "done" is. A task that takes more than a week is a task nobody can see the end of; splitting it is how the team finds the hidden work early, while there is still time.</div>
<p>The team sizes work by time, from the biggest to the smallest. One point is one ideal person-day.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Level</th><th>Size limit</th><th>Tidewater example</th><th>GitLab Free</th><th>GitLab Premium / Ultimate</th><th>Agile kit journal</th></tr>
<tr><td>Project</td><td>years</td><td>The v1 &rarr; v2 port and maintenance, 18 months</td><td>the group <code>tidewater</code></td><td>the group</td><td>one journal: <code>data/tidewater/journal</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Tome</td><td>under 1 year (2 semesters)</td><td>Port Tidewater v1 to SysML v2</td><td>label <code>tome::port</code></td><td>top-level epic (Ultimate: multi-level)</td><td><code>t:</code> on each story</td></tr>
<tr><td>Epic</td><td>under 1 semester (13 sprints)</td><td>Structure port</td><td>issue <code>type::epic</code> + label <code>epic::structure</code></td><td>group epic</td><td><code>e:</code> on each story</td></tr>
<tr><td>Story</td><td>under 1 sprint</td><td>Close the Stations TODO lines</td><td>issue <code>type::story</code> in milestone <em>Sprint 4</em></td><td>issue in an iteration</td><td>an item <code>TW-&lt;issue&gt;</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Task</td><td>under 1 week (&le; 5 points)</td><td>Write <code>state def BuoyModes</code></td><td>issue <code>type::task</code>, "Part of #18"</td><td>task (child work item)</td><td>not recorded: task detail stays in GitLab</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The split between GitLab and the journal follows the kit's ownership rule: the architect's journal records stories,
epics and tomes (what the program steers by); tasks and hours stay in the team's own tool. A story's journal id is its
GitLab issue number, so <code>TW-18</code> in a stand-up is <code>#18</code> in GitLab.</p>
<div class="note">On Free, an epic is an ordinary issue carrying <code>type::epic</code>, plus a label its stories carry.
The epic issue's task list (<code>- [ ] #18</code>) shows progress as "3 of 7 checklist items". On Premium, move the
text into a group epic and attach the stories; nothing else in the process changes.</div>

<h2 id="setup">Setting up the project</h2>
<div class="why"><b>The objection:</b> "Somebody clicks through the settings once and we are done." <b>Why bother:</b> the settings <em>are</em> the configuration control rules: who may change the master copy, who approves, what is audited. Kept in a file, they are reviewed, versioned and re-applied like any controlled document, and you can prove at any time that the server still matches them.</div>
<p>A project is a set of settings as much as a repository. <code>bin/gitlab.pl setup</code> applies them from one file,
so they are reviewed like code, rebuilt in minutes, and compared with the server at any time. Re-running it is safe: it
creates what is missing and updates what differs.</p>
<pre><code># against the real server: a token with the api scope, in an environment variable
export GITLAB_TOKEN=...                       # never on the command line, never in a file in the repo
perl bin/gitlab.pl --url https://gitlab.example.com --token-env GITLAB_TOKEN --dry-run setup tools/gitlab/templates/project.conf
perl bin/gitlab.pl --url https://gitlab.example.com --token-env GITLAB_TOKEN setup tools/gitlab/templates/project.conf

# offline: the same commands against the emulator (a directory)
perl bin/gitlab.pl --local data/tidewater/gitlab setup tools/gitlab/templates/project.conf
perl bin/gitlab.pl --local data/tidewater/gitlab --project tidewater/model sprints 2026-10-05 39</code></pre>
<p><code>--dry-run</code> prints every API call it would make and changes nothing; read it the first time. The
offline emulator (<code>--local DIR</code>) keeps the project state in <code>DIR/gitlab.json</code> and the repository
as a bare Git repo, with a server-side hook that enforces the branch and tag protections. It is how this kit tests the
scripts and runs the simulation, and how you can rehearse a change to the settings before applying it.</p>
<p>The project file for Tidewater. Lines are <code>key = value</code>; a <code>;</code> starts a comment (not
<code>#</code>, which label colours use):</p>
<!--#include templates/project.conf-->
<h3>The order, the first time</h3>
<p>Protecting <code>main</code> with <code>push:none</code> also stops the very first push, so the skeleton goes in before
the protections:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Create an empty project <code>tidewater/model</code> (New project &gt; Create blank project, without a README), or let
      <code>setup</code> create it from a copy of the file that holds only <code>[project]</code>.</li>
  <li>Push the skeleton to <code>main</code> and create <code>develop</code>:
<pre><code>git clone https://gitlab.example.com/tidewater/model.git &amp;&amp; cd model
cp $KIT/tools/gitlab/templates/gitlab-ci.yml .gitlab-ci.yml
cp $KIT/tools/gitlab/templates/{CODEOWNERS,README.md,CHANGELOG.md} .
cp -r $KIT/tools/gitlab/templates/.gitlab .
git add -A &amp;&amp; git commit -m "Project skeleton: pipeline, templates, owners" &amp;&amp; git push -u origin main
perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl flow init            # develop from main, pushed</code></pre></li>
  <li>Apply the whole file: <code>gitlab.pl setup project.conf</code>. From here on <code>main</code> and <code>develop</code>
      take changes only by merge request, and <code>develop</code> is the default branch.</li>
  <li>Register a runner (<a href="#admin">Administration</a>) and check that the CI/CD variable <code>KIT</code> points at
      the kit's checkout on it.</li>
  <li>Create the weekly pipeline schedule (Build &gt; Pipeline schedules, Mondays 06:00) and a masked CI/CD variable
      <code>STATUS_TOKEN</code> holding a <code>read_api</code> project access token.</li>
  <li>Put the wiki pages from <code>tools/gitlab/templates/wiki/</code>, one command per page:
      <code>gitlab.pl wiki put "Definition of Done" "templates/wiki/Definition of Done.md"</code>.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="gitflow">Gitflow</h2>
<div class="why"><b>The objection:</b> "We had one master copy and it worked." <b>Why bother:</b> you still have one master copy (<code>develop</code>). Branches are the working copies everyone always made anyway, on their own drives and in e-mail attachments, except that Git knows about them and can bring the changes back in exactly. The baselines get their own branch (<code>main</code>) so a release never waits on, or is disturbed by, the work in progress.</div>
<p>The model is <em>released</em>: analyses and reviews cite a baseline, and a baseline must never change. Day-to-day
work must never wait on a release either. Gitflow gives each its own branch: <code>main</code> is exactly the list of
baselines, <code>develop</code> is where the work integrates, and short-lived branches carry each change. Tidewater
adds one branch gitflow does not have, <code>vendor/prime</code>, for the prime's drops.</p>

<figure>
<svg viewBox="0 0 900 290" role="img" aria-label="Gitflow branches for the Tidewater model" font-family="Calibri, Segoe UI, Arial, sans-serif" font-size="12">
  <g font-weight="600" fill="#1a1a1a">
    <text x="8" y="34">vendor/prime</text><text x="8" y="84">main</text><text x="8" y="134">release/*, hotfix/*</text>
    <text x="8" y="184">develop</text><text x="8" y="234">feature/*</text>
  </g>
  <g stroke-width="3" fill="none">
    <line x1="120" y1="30" x2="880" y2="30" stroke="#808082"/>
    <line x1="120" y1="80" x2="880" y2="80" stroke="#27235d"/>
    <line x1="170" y1="180" x2="880" y2="180" stroke="#e6af22"/>
    <line x1="580" y1="130" x2="640" y2="130" stroke="#d6292e"/>
    <line x1="740" y1="130" x2="790" y2="130" stroke="#d6292e"/>
