<figcaption>Figure 1. The Tidewater branches over the first baseline. Time runs left to right.</figcaption>
</figure>

<table>
<tr><th>Branch</th><th>Created from</th><th>Merges into</th><th>Who pushes</th><th>Who merges</th><th>Lifetime</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>main</code></td><td>(the first commit)</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>nobody</td><td>maintainers: Jim, Tom</td><td>forever; baselines only</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>develop</code></td><td><code>main</code></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>nobody</td><td>developers, after approval</td><td>forever; the default branch</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>feature/&lt;iid&gt;-&lt;slug&gt;</code></td><td><code>develop</code></td><td><code>develop</code></td><td>its author</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>one task or story; deleted on merge</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>release/baseline-N</code></td><td><code>develop</code></td><td><code>main</code> (then back-merge)</td><td>maintainers</td><td>maintainers</td><td>days</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>hotfix/&lt;name&gt;</code></td><td><code>main</code></td><td><code>main</code> and <code>develop</code></td><td>maintainers</td><td>maintainers</td><td>hours</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>vendor/prime</code></td><td>the first commit of <code>main</code></td><td><code>develop</code>, through <code>feature/drop-&lt;version&gt;</code></td><td>Tom only</td><td>nobody</td><td>forever; never edited by hand</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Rules the team keeps</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Name feature branches after the issue</strong>: <code>feature/19-stations-level-tags</code>. GitLab links the
      branch to issue 19, and the MR gets "Closes #19" from the template.</li>
  <li><strong>Merge commits, never squash or rebase-merge.</strong> The merge history is evidence: who changed which
      package when, how long an MR was open, how many merges needed a fix-forward. The status metrics read it.</li>
  <li><strong><code>develop</code> is the default branch.</strong> GitLab closes issues named by "Closes #N" only when the
      MR merges into the default branch; with gitflow that is <code>develop</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>A feature branch lives less than a week</strong>, like the task it carries. Longer branches drift from the
      prime's drops and conflict.</li>
  <li><strong>Releases and hotfixes back-merge</strong> into <code>develop</code> the same day, so a fix is never lost.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The commands</h3>
<p><code>gitlab.pl flow</code> does the branch bookkeeping and opens the merge requests, from the current clone:</p>
<pre><code>perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl flow start feature 19-stations-level-tags   # from an up-to-date develop
# ... edit, commit ...
perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl flow publish                                 # push, and the MR pipeline starts once the MR exists
perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl flow finish --title "Replace the Stations level-tag TODOs" \
     --template .gitlab/merge_request_templates/Default.md          # MR to develop

perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl flow start release baseline-1                # from develop
perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl flow finish --template .gitlab/merge_request_templates/Release.md   # MR to main;
#   after it merges: tag baseline-1, release, back-merge MR main -> develop

perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl flow start hotfix cell-level-tag             # from main
perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl flow sync                                    # fetch; fast-forward develop and main</code></pre>
<p>The same with plain Git, for when you want to see each step:</p>
<pre><code>git switch develop &amp;&amp; git pull --ff-only
git switch -c feature/19-stations-level-tags
git commit -am "Replace the Stations level-tag TODOs with @L tags" -m "Changelog: changed" -m "Issue: #19"
git push -u origin feature/19-stations-level-tags \
    -o merge_request.create -o merge_request.target=develop \
    -o merge_request.title="Replace the Stations level-tag TODOs" -o merge_request.label="workflow::review"</code></pre>
<p>The <code>-o merge_request.*</code> <em>push options</em> make GitLab open the MR as part of the push, with no token
and no web page. They are a quick alternative to <code>flow finish</code>, without the template.</p>

<h2 id="protect">Protected branches and tags</h2>
<div class="why"><b>The objection:</b> "We trust our people." <b>Why bother:</b> protection is not about trust; it is about accidents. One stray save into the master copy, one baseline overwritten, used to cost a week of reconstruction. Here the server refuses it, for everyone including the project lead, so nobody has to remember the rule.</div>
<p>Protection is what turns the branch table above from a convention into a rule. Settings &gt; Repository &gt; Protected
branches and Protected tags; <code>gitlab.pl setup</code> applies the <code>[branches]</code> and <code>[tags]</code>
sections.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value for <code>main</code> / <code>develop</code></th><th>What it prevents</th></tr>
<tr><td>Allowed to push and merge</td><td>No one (push); maintainers / developers (merge)</td><td>A change that skipped review and the pipeline</td></tr>
<tr><td>Allowed to force push</td><td>off</td><td>Rewriting history that baselines, MRs and metrics point at</td></tr>
<tr><td>Require approval from code owners</td><td>on</td><td>A package changing without its owner seeing it (Premium)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Protected tag <code>baseline-*</code>, allowed to create</td><td>maintainers</td><td>A baseline created, moved or deleted by mistake: protected tags cannot be updated or deleted from Git</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pipelines must succeed (merge checks)</td><td>on</td><td>A merge with a red or missing pipeline</td></tr>
<tr><td>All threads must be resolved</td><td>on</td><td>A merge with an open review question</td></tr>
</table>
<p>In the offline emulator the same rules live in a <code>pre-receive</code> hook in the bare repository. A direct push
to <code>develop</code> is refused the same way GitLab refuses it:</p>
<pre><code>remote: GitLab: You are not allowed to push code to protected branches on this project.
 ! [remote rejected] develop -&gt; develop (pre-receive hook declined)</code></pre>

<h2 id="owners">Code owners and approvals</h2>
<div class="why"><b>The objection:</b> "The CCB meets monthly; that is our approval." <b>Why bother:</b> a monthly board batches a month of changes into one meeting nobody can fully read. Code owners are the CCB split by package and available every day: each change is small enough to read in minutes, and it is approved by the person who knows that part of the model. The monthly board can then review baselines and decisions instead of redlines.</div>
<p><code>CODEOWNERS</code> says who must approve a change to which path. GitLab reads it from the MR's
<strong>target</strong> branch, so a change to the file itself only takes effect once it is merged. Sections let one MR
need approvals from several owners: a change to Stations and to the requirements needs Bob or Jim <em>and</em> Jim.</p>
<!--#include templates/CODEOWNERS-->
<div class="note">On GitLab Free the owners are shown on each file and MR, but "require approval from code owners" and
approval rules need Premium. On Free, the team keeps the rule by convention: the MR template assigns the reviewer, and
the merge is done by someone other than the author.</div>
<p>Approval rules (Premium) add a project-wide minimum on top: <code>Model review = 1 jim bob</code> means one approval
from Jim or Bob on every MR. Set <em>Prevent approval by author</em> and <em>Remove all approvals when commits are added
to the source branch</em> under Settings &gt; Merge requests.</p>

<h2 id="mr">Merge requests</h2>
<div class="why"><b>The objection:</b> "An ECP takes weeks; this sounds like more paperwork." <b>Why bother:</b> a merge request is the change request, the redlines, the review comments, the audit results and the approval signature in one page that writes itself from the work. It is less paperwork, not more: nobody assembles a package, and the record cannot be lost or backdated.</div>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Open it early as a draft</strong> (<code>Draft:</code> title prefix, or <code>/draft</code>). The pipeline
      runs on every push, and the reviewer can follow along.</li>
  <li><strong>Fill the template.</strong> <em>Default</em> for a task, <em>Prime Drop</em> for a drop merge,
      <em>Release</em> and <em>Hotfix</em> for those branches. The template carries "Closes #N", the checks list, and
      quick actions that set the label and reviewer.</li>
  <li><strong>The pipeline runs on the merged result</strong>: the source merged into the current target, the commit
      that would exist after the merge (Premium calls this merged results pipelines; the emulator always does it).</li>
  <li><strong>Review in threads.</strong> Comment on a line; propose a change with a <code>suggestion</code> block the
      author applies with one click; resolve each thread when it is answered.</li>
  <li><strong>Approve, then merge.</strong> The merge checks are: not a draft, pipeline succeeded, threads resolved,
      approvals met (code owners included). GitLab writes the merge commit as
      <code>Merge branch 'feature/19-…' into 'develop'</code>, with the title, "Closes #19" and "See merge request
      tidewater/model!19".</li>
</ol>
<p>A filled-in MR description and its review thread are in <a href="#markdown">Markdown, field by field</a>.</p>

<h2 id="cicd">CI/CD from zero</h2>
<div class="why"><b>The objection:</b> "CI/CD is for software. We build models and documents." <b>Why bother:</b> strip
the software words away and CI/CD is two habits a configuration manager already values. One is checking every change
the day it is made. The other is keeping the deliverables ready to hand over at any moment. The machine does both, so
people stop doing them by hand at the end.</div>

<h3>CI: continuous integration, in systems engineering words</h3>
<p><strong>Integration</strong> is bringing one person's change into the master copy. <strong>Continuous</strong> means
it happens every day, in small pieces, not in a big merge before a review. Each change is <strong>checked
automatically</strong> before it goes in. In the words you know, CI is a <em>configuration audit plus the peer-review
checklist, run by the server on every single change</em>.</p>
<p>For Tidewater, every merge request is checked in a few minutes:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Every file is valid SysML v2 text (<code>grammar</code>).</li>
  <li>Every name points at something that exists (<code>lint</code>).</li>
  <li>The part tree keeps its shape and level tags (<code>outline</code>).</li>
  <li>No secret was committed (<code>secrets</code>).</li>
  <li>Requirements still trace (<code>trace</code>).</li>
  <li>The generated documents match the model (<code>docs</code>).</li>
</ul>

<h3>CD: continuous delivery</h3>
<p><strong>Delivery</strong> means producing the products of the model. For Tidewater those are the generated documents,
the diagrams, the trace report, the model web site, the release notes and the baselines. <strong>Continuous</strong>
means the pipeline produces them from the model on every merge, so they are always current and ready to hand over. Nobody
assembles a delivery package the week before a review. When GitLab talks about <strong>deploy</strong>, here it only
means two things: publishing the model web site (the <code>pages</code> job) and creating a baseline release (the
<code>publish-release</code> job).</p>

<h3>The anatomy of a pipeline</h3>
<figure>
<svg viewBox="0 0 900 250" role="img" aria-label="The Tidewater pipeline: stages left to right, jobs in each stage" font-family="Calibri, Segoe UI, Arial, sans-serif" font-size="12">
  <g font-weight="600" fill="#27235d">
    <text x="20" y="22">check</text><text x="200" y="22">trace</text><text x="340" y="22">docs</text>
    <text x="480" y="22">delta</text><text x="620" y="22">release</text><text x="760" y="22">publish / report</text>
  </g>
  <g fill="#fff" stroke="#27235d" stroke-width="1.5">
    <rect x="20" y="34" width="140" height="28" rx="14"/><rect x="20" y="70" width="140" height="28" rx="14"/>
    <rect x="20" y="106" width="140" height="28" rx="14"/><rect x="20" y="142" width="140" height="28" rx="14"/>
    <rect x="20" y="178" width="140" height="28" rx="14"/>
    <rect x="200" y="34" width="110" height="28" rx="14" stroke="#e6af22"/>
    <rect x="340" y="34" width="110" height="28" rx="14"/>
    <rect x="480" y="34" width="110" height="28" rx="14" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><rect x="480" y="70" width="110" height="28" rx="14" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>
    <rect x="620" y="34" width="120" height="28" rx="14" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><rect x="620" y="70" width="120" height="28" rx="14" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>
    <rect x="760" y="34" width="120" height="28" rx="14" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><rect x="760" y="70" width="120" height="28" rx="14" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>
  </g>
  <g fill="#1a1a1a">
    <text x="36" y="53">✓ grammar</text><text x="36" y="89">✓ lint</text><text x="36" y="125">✓ outline</text>
    <text x="36" y="161">✓ todo</text><text x="36" y="197">✓ secrets</text>
    <text x="214" y="53">✓ trace</text><text x="354" y="53">✓ docs</text>
    <text x="494" y="53">delta</text><text x="494" y="89">drop-package</text>
    <text x="634" y="53">baseline</text><text x="634" y="89">publish-release</text>
    <text x="774" y="53">pages</text><text x="774" y="89">status</text>
  </g>
  <g stroke="#808082" stroke-width="1.5" fill="none"><path d="M160 48 H200"/><path d="M310 48 H340"/><path d="M450 48 H480"/><path d="M590 48 H620"/><path d="M740 48 H760"/></g>
  <g font-size="11" fill="#555">
    <text x="20" y="232">✓ passed   dashed: skipped by its rules in this merge request pipeline</text>
  </g>
</svg>
<figcaption>Figure 2. A Tidewater merge request pipeline, as GitLab draws it (Build &gt; Pipelines, then a pipeline).
Stages run left to right, and the jobs in a stage run together.</figcaption>
</figure>
<table>
<tr><th>Term</th><th>What it is</th><th>Tidewater example</th></tr>
<tr><td>pipeline</td><td>Every check and product for one change, run by the server</td><td>The pipeline of MR !19</td></tr>
<tr><td>stage</td><td>A group of jobs that run together. The stages run in order, and a failure stops the later ones</td><td><code>check</code>, then <code>trace</code>, then <code>docs</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>job</td><td>One check, or one product</td><td><code>lint</code>, <code>pages</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>script</td><td>The commands a job runs. The job fails if a command fails</td><td><code>perl ci/job.pl lint</code> (the job's logic is in that Perl script, so it runs the same in PowerShell, Git Bash or Linux)</td></tr>
<tr><td>runner</td><td>The machine that runs the jobs</td><td>A Windows PC with Git for Windows (<a href="#admin">Administration</a>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>rules</td><td>When a job runs, and when it is skipped</td><td><code>delta</code> runs only for drop branches; <code>baseline</code> only on <code>baseline-*</code> tags</td></tr>
<tr><td>artifacts</td><td>Files a job keeps after it ends, for download or for later jobs</td><td><code>todo.txt</code>, <code>trace.txt</code>, <code>public/</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>reports</td><td>Artifacts in a format GitLab draws on the merge request</td><td>JUnit, Code Quality and metrics from <code>ci-reports.pl</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>environment</td><td>A named place the pipeline delivers to, with a record of what is there now</td><td><code>model-site</code>, the Pages site</td></tr>
<tr><td>variables</td><td>Named settings the jobs read. They can be masked and protected for secrets</td><td><code>KIT</code> (the kit's path); <code>STATUS_TOKEN</code> (masked)</td></tr>
<tr><td>cache</td><td>Files kept between pipelines to save time</td><td>Not needed here: the checks take seconds</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>allow_failure</code></td><td>The job may fail without blocking the merge. It shows yellow</td><td><code>trace</code> started this way in sprint 3; since sprint 4 it is a ratchet instead</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>when: manual</code></td><td>The job waits for a person to press its play button</td><td>Could guard a release; Tidewater uses protected tags instead</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>What starts a pipeline</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Trigger</th><th>When</th><th>What runs for Tidewater</th></tr>
<tr><td>merge request</td><td>An MR is opened or updated</td><td>The checks, on the <em>merged result</em>: your branch merged into the current <code>develop</code>, so you test what will actually exist after the merge</td></tr>
<tr><td>push</td><td>A branch without an MR changes (<code>develop</code> after a merge, <code>vendor/prime</code>)</td><td>The checks, <code>pages</code> on <code>develop</code>, <code>delta</code> and <code>drop-package</code> on the vendor branch</td></tr>
<tr><td>tag</td><td>A <code>baseline-*</code> tag is created</td><td><code>baseline</code> (the rebuild audit), then <code>publish-release</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>schedule</td><td>A timer, set under Build &gt; Pipeline schedules</td><td><code>status</code>, every Friday; the nightly full check (<code>FULL=1</code>) on weekdays</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Reading a pipeline and a job log</h3>
<p>On the MR page, the pipeline line shows one icon per stage: green is passed, red is failed, yellow is failed but
allowed, grey is skipped. Click a red icon, then the red job, to open its log. A log reads top to bottom, like this one
with notes added on the right:</p>
<pre><code>Running with gitlab-runner 17.2 on tidewater-win            &lt;- which runner took the job
Fetching changes with git depth set to 0...                 &lt;- the runner gets the merged result
Checking out 3c9e1a2f as detached HEAD (ref is refs/merge-requests/19/merge)
$ perl ci/job.pl lint                                       &lt;- the job's one script line
$ /usr/bin/perl /opt/agile/tools/sysml/model.pl lint        &lt;- what ci/job.pl runs, printed before it runs it
model/physical/stations/Stations.sysml:47:9: error: unresolved type 'Hygrometr'   &lt;- the finding: file, line, column, message
lint: 13 file(s), 1 error(s), 0 warning(s)
elements=139 errors=1 warnings=0                            &lt;- the line the gate reads
$ /usr/bin/perl /opt/agile/tools/gitlab/ci-reports.pl lint --junit lint.xml ...
ci-reports lint: 2 check(s), 2 failed, 1 finding(s); ...    &lt;- reports for the MR widgets
lint is not 0/0 (elements=139 errors=1 warnings=0)          &lt;- why the job failed
Uploading artifacts... lint.txt lint.xml lint-cq.json       &lt;- kept even on failure (when: always)
ERROR: Job failed: exit code 1                              &lt;- a non-zero exit fails the job</code></pre>
<p>The fix is in the line that names a file and a line number. Open it in Vim with <code>vim +47 model/physical/stations/Stations.sysml</code>.</p>

<h3>Running it on your own machine first</h3>
<pre><code>perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl ci --list --event merge_request_event --branch feature/19-stations --target develop
#   which jobs would run for this MR, and the rule that skips each of the others
perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl ci --event merge_request_event --branch feature/19-stations --target develop --var KIT=$KIT
#   runs them here, stage by stage, with the variables GitLab would set</code></pre>

<h3>Shift left: catch it at the merge, not at integration</h3>
<p>"Shift left" means moving a check earlier in time, to the left on a schedule chart. An interface mismatch found by
<code>lint</code> on the day it was typed costs the author five minutes. The same mismatch found at a design review
months later costs a meeting, a defect report and rework of everything built on it. Status metric H counts this
directly: the mismatches caught at merge, against those that reached integration.</p>

<h3>The Sec in DevSecOps</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Secret detection</strong> on every change: the <code>secrets</code> job and GitLab's own template. A
      committed token is caught before it is copied into every clone.</li>
  <li><strong>Protected, masked variables</strong> for credentials. A <em>masked</em> variable is hidden in job logs. A
      <em>protected</em> variable is given only to pipelines on protected branches and tags, so a feature branch cannot
      read it.</li>
  <li><strong>A least-privilege runner.</strong> The runner gets a normal user account, not an administrator. It is
      registered to this project only and tagged, so other projects' jobs never run on it. It holds no credentials beyond
      the job's own token.</li>
  <li><strong>Least-privilege tokens.</strong> Each token gets <code>read_api</code> unless it must write, and an expiry
      date.</li>
  <li><strong>Protected branches and tags</strong>, so nothing reaches the master copy or a baseline without the
      pipeline and an approval.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Growing a pipeline one job at a time</h3>
<p>Do not write the whole file on day one. Add one job, live with it for a sprint, then add the next. Each job should
answer a question a reviewer used to answer by eye.</p>
<ol>
  <li><code>grammar</code>: is every file valid SysML v2 text?</li>
  <li><code>lint</code>: do all the names resolve? Hold it at 0/0 from the first day.</li>
  <li><code>secrets</code>: is nothing secret committed?</li>
  <li><code>docs</code>: do the generated documents match the model?</li>
  <li><code>trace</code>: do the requirements still trace? Start it as <code>allow_failure</code>, then make it a ratchet (fail only on new gaps).</li>
  <li>The reports (JUnit, Code Quality), so failures show on the MR.</li>
  <li><code>pages</code>, <code>baseline</code>, <code>publish-release</code>, and the weekly <code>status</code>.</li>
</ol>

<h3>The minimum pipeline for any SysML v2 text repository</h3>
<p>Ten lines, enough to start. Set <code>KIT</code> as a CI/CD variable and copy
<code>tools/gitlab/templates/ci/job.pl</code> to <code>ci/job.pl</code>. In the template, the <code>workflow</code> block
and the rest come later.</p>
<pre><code>stages:
  - check

grammar:
  stage: check
  script:
    - perl ci/job.pl grammar

lint:
  stage: check
  script:
    - perl ci/job.pl lint</code></pre>
<p>Why <code>perl ci/job.pl NAME</code> and not shell lines: GitLab Runner's Windows shell executor runs PowerShell,
where <code>$KIT</code> is <code>$env:KIT</code> and <code>$(find ...)</code>, <code>[ -f ... ]</code>, pipes and quoting
behave differently. A Perl script behaves the same in PowerShell, cmd, Git Bash, a Linux runner, the Docker image and
<code>gitlab.pl ci</code> on a laptop.</p>

<h2 id="pipeline">The pipeline, job by job</h2>
<div class="why"><b>The objection:</b> "We audit at the baseline, like always." <b>Why bother:</b> an audit at the end finds a year of problems at once, when they are hardest to fix. The pipeline is the same configuration audit, written down once and run by the server on every change, in minutes. Problems are found the day they are made, by the person who made them, before they reach the master copy. Nobody spends a week preparing for it.</div>
<p>The pipeline is the part of the process nobody can skip. Each job checks one thing a reviewer would otherwise have
to check by eye. Every job's script is one line, <code>perl ci/job.pl NAME</code>; <code>ci/job.pl</code> (committed in the
model repo, from <code>tools/gitlab/templates/ci/job.pl</code>) calls the kit through the CI/CD variable <code>KIT</code>.
Nothing is installed on the runner beyond Git for Windows (or any Perl 5 with Git).</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Stage</th><th>Job</th><th>Runs on</th><th>Checks</th><th>Fails the MR?</th></tr>
<tr><td>check</td><td><code>grammar</code></td><td>every change</td><td>Every <code>.sysml</code> file parses against the official SysML v2 grammar</td><td>yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>check</td><td><code>lint</code></td><td>every change</td><td>Names resolve, imports are used; the last line must be <code>errors=0 warnings=0</code></td><td>yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>check</td><td><code>outline</code></td><td>every change</td><td>2 to 9 children at every level; each part def has its level tag</td><td>only if the change adds a violation (a ratchet against <code>.ci/outline-max</code>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>check</td><td><code>todo</code></td><td>every change</td><td>Counts <code>// TODO</code> lines: the port burn-down (artifact <code>todo.txt</code>)</td><td>never</td></tr>
<tr><td>check</td><td><code>secrets</code></td><td>every change</td><td>No access tokens, cloud keys or private keys in the tree</td><td>yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>trace</td><td><code>trace</code></td><td>every change</td><td>DOORS against the model: linked, orphan, unverified, drifted requirements</td><td>only if the change adds a gap (a ratchet against the merge base); reports only on <code>develop</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>docs</td><td><code>docs</code></td><td>every change</td><td>Generated docs are committed and current</td><td>yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>delta</td><td><code>delta</code></td><td><code>vendor/prime</code>, <code>feature/drop-*</code> MRs</td><td>What the prime changed in v1 since the previous drop; which changes need hand rework</td><td>only on error</td></tr>
<tr><td>release</td><td><code>baseline</code></td><td>tags <code>baseline-*</code></td><td>The tagged tree rebuilds its docs byte for byte, the hash matches the tag message; writes <code>toolchain.txt</code> (kit, Perl, Git, OS, runner, image)</td><td>yes (the tag stays, the release is held)</td></tr>
<tr><td>delta</td><td><code>drop-package</code></td><td><code>vendor/prime</code></td><td>Files the drop, as delivered, in the generic package registry (<code>prime-drop</code>, by version)</td><td>only on error</td></tr>
<tr><td>release</td><td><code>publish-release</code></td><td>tags <code>baseline-*</code>, after <code>baseline</code></td><td>Creates the GitLab release with the baseline's CHANGELOG section and the toolchain as notes</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
<tr><td>publish</td><td><code>pages</code></td><td>the default branch</td><td>Publishes the model's web site: generated docs, tree / trace / package views, the trace summary, the TODO burn-down</td><td>never blocks a merge (runs after it)</td></tr>
<tr><td>report</td><td><code>status</code></td><td>the weekly schedule</td><td>Exports issues, MRs and jobs for the status metrics</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Words used in the table: a <em>job</em> is one check; a <em>stage</em> is a group of jobs that run together, in the order
listed; a <em>runner</em> is the machine that runs them; an <em>artifact</em> is a file a job keeps (a report, a count);
a <em>rule</em> says when a job runs.</p>
<h3>What the merge request shows: reports</h3>
<p>Each check job also writes its results in the formats GitLab draws on the merge request page, using
<code>tools/gitlab/ci-reports.pl</code> on the checkers' own output:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Report</th><th>Shown as</th><th>Written by</th><th>Tier</th></tr>
<tr><td>JUnit XML (<code>reports:junit</code>)</td><td>the test summary: each failed check with its message (one case per model file for grammar and lint; one per requirement gap for trace)</td><td><code>ci-reports.pl grammar|lint|outline|trace --junit</code></td><td>Free</td></tr>
<tr><td>Code Quality JSON (<code>reports:codequality</code>)</td><td>the findings this change added or fixed: lint errors and warnings, outline violations, TODO lines (as <em>info</em>)</td><td><code>ci-reports.pl ... --codequality</code></td><td>Free (widget), Premium (inline in the diff)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Metrics (<code>reports:metrics</code>)</td><td>how each number moved against the target branch: <code>todo_lines</code>, <code>lint_errors</code>, <code>trace_completeness_pct</code>, &hellip;</td><td><code>ci-reports.pl ... --metrics</code></td><td>Premium</td></tr>
</table>
<pre><code>perl ci/job.pl lint      # runs, in turn:
perl $KIT/tools/sysml/model.pl lint &gt; lint.txt 2&gt;&amp;1
perl $KIT/tools/gitlab/ci-reports.pl lint --junit lint.xml --codequality lint-cq.json --metrics lint.metrics lint.txt
ci-reports lint: 1 check(s), 0 failed, 0 finding(s); model_elements=138 lint_errors=0 lint_warnings=0</code></pre>
<p>A Code Quality finding's <em>fingerprint</em> is a hash of its file, check and message (not its line), so a finding
keeps its identity when lines above it move, and GitLab can tell "fixed" from "moved".</p>
<h3>Why these rules</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong><code>workflow:rules</code></strong> give one pipeline per change: an MR gets a merge request pipeline, and
      the same branch does not also get a branch pipeline. Without it every push to an MR runs twice.</li>
  <li><strong>The trace job is a ratchet.</strong> Requirement gaps are often the prime's to fix (a discrepancy
      report); blocking every unrelated MR on them would stop the team without fixing anything. So on a merge request the
      job also traces the model as it was at the merge base, and fails only when the change <em>adds</em> a gap: removes
      a satisfy, drops a verify, edits a requirement's text away from DOORS. The existing gaps stay in the widget.</li>
  <li><strong>Hidden jobs and anchors</strong> (<code>.check</code>, <code>&amp;model_rules</code>) keep the shared rules in
      one place: the weekly status schedule runs only the report; the nightly schedule (<code>FULL=1</code>) runs the
      full check and refreshes the site.</li>
  <li><strong><code>include: template: Security/Secret-Detection.gitlab-ci.yml</code></strong> adds GitLab's own secret
      scanner, but only when the project variable <code>DOCKER_RUNNER</code> is <code>"true"</code> (the include has its
      own <code>rules:</code>). The scanner runs in a Docker image; on the team's Windows shell runner its job would wait
      forever and, with "pipelines must succeed", block every MR. The <code>secrets</code> job is the always-on check: a scan
      of every tracked file in <code>ci/job.pl</code>, which runs on any runner and on a laptop.</li>
  <li><strong><code>default: tags: [windows, mbse]</code></strong> sends the jobs to the team's Windows shell runner. On a
      Docker runner (<code>tools/gitlab/docker</code>) drop the tags or use that runner's own.</li>
  <li><strong>The outline job is a ratchet.</strong> The converted model inherits outline violations from the prime's
      structure, most of them fixable only upstream. <code>.ci/outline-max</code> holds the count allowed: a change that adds
      a violation fails; a change that removes one lowers the number in the same MR. At 0 the rule is absolute.</li>
</ul>
<!--#include templates/gitlab-ci.yml-->
<p>And the script every job runs, <code>ci/job.pl</code>:</p>
<!--#include templates/ci/job.pl-->

<h2 id="local">Running the pipeline locally</h2>
<div class="why"><b>The objection:</b> "Why run it myself if the server runs it anyway?" <b>Why bother:</b> it is the desk check before the review. A red check found on your laptop costs a minute; found on the server, it costs a review round and someone else's attention.</div>
<p><code>gitlab.pl ci</code> reads <code>.gitlab-ci.yml</code>, applies GitLab's rules for the event you name, and runs
the jobs in the current directory, stage by stage, with the predefined variables GitLab would set. Run it before every
push: a red job found on the laptop costs a minute; found on the server, it costs a review round.</p>
<pre><code>cd model                                    # the model repo clone
export KIT=/c/Users/you/agile
perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl ci --list --event merge_request_event --branch feature/19-stations --target develop
perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl ci --event merge_request_event --branch feature/19-stations --target develop --var KIT=$KIT
perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl ci --job lint       # one job
perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl ci --event tag --tag baseline-1   # what the tag pipeline will do</code></pre>
<p><code>--list</code> prints which jobs would run and, for the others, which rule skipped them. Included templates are
reported as "runs on GitLab only". The local runner understands the subset of the CI syntax the Tidewater pipeline uses:
<code>stages</code>, <code>variables</code>, <code>default</code>, <code>workflow:rules</code>, <code>include</code>
(reported), hidden jobs, <code>extends</code>, YAML anchors and <code>&lt;&lt;</code> merges, <code>rules</code>
(<code>if</code>, <code>changes</code>, <code>exists</code>, <code>when</code>, <code>allow_failure</code>),
<code>only</code>/<code>except</code>, <code>needs</code>, <code>artifacts:paths</code>. Keys it does not need
(<code>image</code>, <code>services</code>, <code>tags</code>, <code>cache</code>, <code>retry</code>) are accepted and
ignored.</p>

<h2 id="issues">Issues, labels, milestones and boards</h2>
<div class="why"><b>The objection:</b> "Our action-item spreadsheet works." <b>Why bother:</b> the spreadsheet works until two people edit it, or until someone asks which change closed an action. An issue is an action item that links to the change that closes it, closes itself when that change is approved, and shows on a board that is always current. Nobody updates a tracker before a meeting.</div>
<h3>Milestones are sprints</h3>
<p>One milestone per sprint, <em>Sprint 1</em> to <em>Sprint 39</em>, two weeks each, created at once by the
<code>[milestones]</code> line of the project file or by <code>gitlab.pl sprints 2026-10-05 39</code>. A story belongs
to exactly one sprint; if it does not finish, it moves to the next milestone and the move is visible in its history (the
carryover the journal counts). Premium has <em>iterations</em> with automatic cadences, which do the same job.</p>
<h3>Labels</h3>
<p>Labels carry everything a milestone cannot: the type of item, its tome and epic, its place on the board, who fixes
it. Scoped labels (<code>key::value</code>) are mutually exclusive on Premium: adding <code>workflow::review</code>
removes <code>workflow::in-progress</code>. On Free the names still group and filter; keep one per scope by habit.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Scope</th><th>Values</th><th>Used for</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>type::</code></td><td>epic, story, task, defect, discrepancy, drop, spike</td><td>what the item is; templates set it</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tome::</code>, <code>epic::</code></td><td>environment, port, maintain; gitlab-project &hellip; sustainment</td><td>the hierarchy above the story</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>workflow::</code></td><td>ready, in-progress, review, blocked, done</td><td>the board's columns</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>priority::</code>, <code>severity::</code></td><td>1 to 3</td><td>order; how bad a defect or DR is</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>owner::</code></td><td>gov, prime</td><td>who fixes it: a defect (gov) or a discrepancy report (prime)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>phase::</code></td><td>port, integration, maintenance</td><td>when it was found (status metric H)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>area::</code></td><td>one per model package, plus interface, pipeline, gov</td><td>which package; matches the CODEOWNERS sections</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>Boards</h3>
<p>The <em>Sprint</em> board is scoped to the current milestone, with one list per <code>workflow::</code> label: moving a
card changes the label. The <em>Prime</em> board, scoped to <code>owner::prime</code>, lists the open discrepancy reports
by severity, and Jim reviews it before each drop.</p>
<h3>Quick actions and time tracking</h3>
<p>A line starting with <code>/</code> in a description or comment is a command, run when the text is saved:
<code>/label</code>, <code>/milestone</code>, <code>/assign</code>, <code>/estimate 2d</code>, <code>/spend 4h</code>,
<code>/relate #8</code>, <code>/close</code>, <code>/approve</code>, <code>/merge</code>. Templates end with quick actions,
so a new story is labelled and placed the moment it is created. <code>/estimate</code> on a task is its size in days;
the five-point limit of a task is the rule <code>/estimate</code> makes visible.</p>
<p>From the command line:</p>
<pre><code>perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl issue new "Close the Stations TODO lines" --template .gitlab/issue_templates/Story.md \
     --label type::story --label epic::structure --label tome::port --milestone "Sprint 4" --assign bob
perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl issue new "Write state def BuoyModes" --type task --template .gitlab/issue_templates/Task.md \
     --label type::task --milestone "Sprint 4" --assign bob
perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl issue link 20 18
perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl issue note 18 "Blocked on the trigger question; asked the prime's modeler. /label ~workflow::blocked"
perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl issue list --label type::story --state opened</code></pre>

<h2 id="drops">Prime drops: the vendor branch</h2>
<div class="why"><b>The objection:</b> "The prime owns the model; we just read their drops." <b>Why bother:</b> reading drops by hand is how the government ended up a passenger. Merged through the vendor branch, each drop lands in the government's own model automatically, the government's improvements survive, and the delta report says exactly what the prime changed, including what their diagrams hide. That is what lets the government check the deliverable, not just receive it.</div>
<p>The prime keeps changing its v1 model, during the port and after it. The government's v2 model carries its own
edits on top of the converter's output: closed TODO lines, level tags, hand-written state machines. Regenerating from a
new drop would wipe those edits; editing the new drop by hand would take days per drop. The vendor branch makes Git do
it.</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>vendor/prime</code> holds, for each drop, exactly what the converters make of it: <code>model/</code> from the
      XMI and the ReqIF, and <code>imports/&lt;version&gt;/</code> with the files as delivered. Nobody edits it.</li>
  <li><code>develop</code> holds the same files plus the government's edits.</li>
  <li>A new drop is a new commit on <code>vendor/prime</code>. Merging it into <code>develop</code> is a three-way merge
      whose base is the previous drop: Git applies the prime's changes and keeps the government's edits. It stops only
      where both sides changed the same lines.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl flow vendor /secure/drops/2027-01-08 v1.1
#  vendor/prime: Import prime drop v1.1 (MANIFEST sha256 ...)       pushed; the delta job runs
#  feature/drop-v1.1: merged vendor/prime into develop               conflicts: model/analysis/Analysis.sysml</code></pre>
<p>Two things the merge cannot do, which the process covers:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Changes inside what the converter does not carry.</strong> A new state in a v1 state machine changes
      nothing in the converter's output (the state machine is a single TODO line), so the merge sees no change. The
      <code>delta</code> job compares the two drops' XMI element by element and marks those lines <em>manual</em>; each
      becomes a task.</li>
  <li><strong>Conflicts.</strong> The rule is: take the prime's text, then re-apply the government's edit on top. The
      intake story lists each conflict and its resolution in the MR.</li>
</ol>
<p>The full procedure is the <em>Drop Intake Runbook</em> wiki page (<a href="#wiki">below</a>).</p>

<h2 id="releases">Releases and baselines</h2>
<div class="why"><b>The objection:</b> "We already number our baselines." <b>Why bother:</b> a number on a cover page can be applied to the wrong file. A protected tag points at exactly one state of every file, cannot be moved, and its pipeline proves the baseline rebuilds byte for byte: the physical configuration audit, done by machine.</div>
<p>A baseline is three things that point at one commit: the merge of <code>release/baseline-N</code> into
<code>main</code>, the annotated protected tag <code>baseline-N</code>, and a GitLab release whose notes come from the
CHANGELOG. The tag message carries <code>sha256=</code> of the generated docs; the tag pipeline rebuilds the docs from
the tagged model and checks the hash, so "this baseline rebuilds byte for byte" is checked, not claimed.</p>
<pre><code>perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl flow start release baseline-1
#   move CHANGELOG [Unreleased] under the baseline heading; regenerate docs; commit
perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl flow finish --template .gitlab/merge_request_templates/Release.md
#   after the merge: tag baseline-1, release with notes, back-merge MR main -> develop
perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl release baseline-1 --notes notes.md --name "Tidewater model baseline-1"   # by hand, if needed</code></pre>

<h3>The model's web site (GitLab Pages)</h3>
<p>The <code>pages</code> job rebuilds a small web site on every merge to <code>develop</code>: the generated documents,
the part tree, trace and package views drawn from the model, the requirement trace summary, and the TODO burn-down
(the port's progress, counted at every merge). GitLab serves it at the project's Pages address (Deploy &gt; Pages); set
Pages access control to project members. It is for the program office, the prime and review boards: people who need the
model's current state and will never open a repository. The <em>environment</em> <code>model-site</code> records which
commit the site shows and when it changed. The front page is written by <code>tools/gitlab/pages-index.pl</code>.</p>

<h2 id="wiki">The wiki</h2>
<div class="why"><b>The objection:</b> "We have a SEP and a CM plan." <b>Why bother:</b> keep them; the wiki holds the working procedures that sit under them, short enough to read while doing the work, and changed the same week the team changes how it works, with a history of every change.</div>
<p>The wiki holds what changes slowly and is read often: procedures, definitions and decisions. It is a Git repository
of Markdown pages (<code>model.wiki.git</code>), so it can be cloned, reviewed and backed up like the model. Tidewater
starts with these pages, from <code>tools/gitlab/templates/wiki/</code>:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Page</th><th>Holds</th></tr>
<tr><td>Home</td><td>why the team is doing this, what changes and what stays, the team, the hierarchy, the sprint</td></tr>
<tr><td>Glossary</td><td>systems engineering and CM terms, and their GitLab and DevSecOps names</td></tr>
<tr><td>Definition of Ready / Definition of Done</td><td>when a story enters a sprint; when a task, story or drop is done; commit message rules</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gitflow</td><td>the branch table, the picture, the commands</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drop Intake Runbook</td><td>a prime drop from receipt to merged</td></tr>
<tr><td>Baseline Procedure</td><td>cutting <code>baseline-N</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Onboarding</td><td>a new member's first day and first task, step by step, with what each step is like in the old way</td></tr>
<tr><td>Administration</td><td>what is kept running, how often, by whom</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decision: &hellip;</td><td>one page per decision (a sample is below)</td></tr>
</table>
<details><summary>The Drop Intake Runbook page</summary>
<!--#include templates/wiki/Drop Intake Runbook.md-->
</details>

<h2 id="markdown">Markdown, field by field</h2>
<div class="why"><b>The objection:</b> "Another markup language?" <b>Why bother:</b> Markdown is plain text with a few conventions you have used in e-mail for years: <code>**bold**</code>, <code>- item</code>, tables drawn with <code>|</code>. It works in every GitLab field, and the samples below show everything this team needs. Copy one and change the words.</div>
<p>Every text field in GitLab is GitLab Flavored Markdown: issue and MR descriptions, comments, milestone descriptions,
release notes, wiki pages, even commit messages (rendered in the UI). Below, each field as the Tidewater team fills it in
during sprints 1 to 6. The files are in <code>tools/gitlab/samples/</code>; copy one as a starting point.</p>

<h3>The references every field understands</h3>
<!--#include samples/15-snippet-and-mentions.md-->

<h3>Epic description</h3>
<p>The goal, the scope as an In/Out table, the stories as a task list (so progress shows as "3 of 7"), the measures and
the timeline. On Free this is the description of a <code>type::epic</code> issue.</p>
<!--#include samples/01-epic.md-->

<h3>Story issue</h3>
<p>Why, then "done" as a checklist someone else can verify, then the tasks with their points. The Definition of Ready
sits in a collapsed <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> block so it is there without crowding the page.</p>
<!--#include samples/02-story.md-->

<h3>Task issue</h3>
<p>"Part of #18" links the task to its story. A code block shows the change; <code>/spend</code> records the time.</p>
<!--#include samples/03-task.md-->

<h3>Defect</h3>
<p>What is wrong, the evidence pasted from the job log, what is expected and where that comes from. An alert box
(<code>&gt; [!note]</code>) records the root cause.</p>
<!--#include samples/04-defect.md-->

<h3>Discrepancy report to the prime</h3>
<p>The government does not edit the prime's model. It reports, with facts side by side, one specific request, and a
closure checklist that the next drop's intake ticks.</p>
<!--#include samples/05-discrepancy-report.md-->

<h3>Prime drop intake</h3>
<!--#include samples/06-prime-drop.md-->

<h3>Merge request description</h3>
<p>"Closes #19" closes the task when the MR merges into the default branch. The local pipeline run goes in a collapsed
block, so the reviewer can see it was run.</p>
<!--#include samples/07-merge-request.md-->

<h3>Review thread</h3>
<p>A line comment with a <code>suggestion</code> block (the author applies it with one click), a reply, a related link
by quick action, and the approval. <code>suggestion:-0+1</code> replaces the commented line and the one after it.</p>
<!--#include samples/08-review-thread.md-->

<h3>Issue comments over a sprint</h3>
<p>Comments are where the stand-up meets GitLab: started, blocked and why, unblocked, time spent, done. The quick
actions move the card on the board.</p>
<!--#include samples/09-issue-comments.md-->

<h3>Milestone description (a sprint)</h3>
<!--#include samples/10-milestone.md-->

<h3>Release notes</h3>
<!--#include samples/11-release.md-->

<h3>Wiki page: a decision record</h3>
<p><code>[[_TOC_]]</code>, a status table, a Mermaid diagram and a footnote.</p>
<!--#include samples/12-wiki-decision.md-->

<h3>Commit messages</h3>
<p>Subject in the imperative, a blank line, the why, then trailers. <code>Changelog:</code> trailers build the
CHANGELOG; <code>Issue:</code> and <code>Fixes:</code> link the issue.</p>
<!--#include samples/13-commit-messages.md-->

<h3>Labels and boards</h3>
<!--#include samples/14-board-and-labels.md-->

<h2 id="templates">The templates</h2>
<p>Templates make the fields above the default. GitLab offers every file in <code>.gitlab/issue_templates/</code> and
<code>.gitlab/merge_request_templates/</code> in a drop-down when an issue or MR is created; a template named
<code>Default.md</code> is preselected for MRs. All of them are in <code>tools/gitlab/templates/</code>:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Kind</th><th>Templates</th></tr>
<tr><td>Issue</td><td>Story, Task, Defect, Discrepancy Report, Prime Drop, Epic, Spike</td></tr>
<tr><td>Merge request</td><td>Default, Prime Drop, Release, Hotfix</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wiki</td><td>Home, Definition of Ready, Definition of Done, Gitflow, Drop Intake Runbook, Baseline Procedure, Onboarding, Administration</td></tr>
<tr><td>Repository</td><td><code>gitlab-ci.yml</code>, <code>CODEOWNERS</code>, <code>README.md</code>, <code>CHANGELOG.md</code>, <code>release-notes.md</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Project</td><td><code>project.conf</code> (for <code>gitlab.pl setup</code>)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Each template is mostly comments (<code>&lt;!-- … --&gt;</code>, invisible when rendered) that say what goes where,
and it ends with quick actions that label and place the new item.</p>
<details><summary>Issue template: Story</summary>
<!--#include templates/.gitlab/issue_templates/Story.md-->
</details>
<details><summary>Issue template: Prime Drop</summary>
<!--#include templates/.gitlab/issue_templates/Prime Drop.md-->
</details>
