<h2 id="export">Exports for the status metrics</h2>
<div class="why"><b>The objection:</b> "Status is a briefing chart." <b>Why bother:</b> the chart stays; the numbers on it stop being typed. Every number comes from the work itself, the same way every week, so the briefing becomes a discussion of what the numbers mean rather than an argument about whether they are right.</div>
<p>The A to Z status metrics (<a href="STATUS-METRICS.html">STATUS-METRICS.html</a>) read the model repository and a
folder of exports. <code>gitlab.pl export DIR</code> writes the exports from what GitLab knows; the weekly scheduled
pipeline runs it, and it works the same against the offline emulator.</p>
<pre><code>perl $KIT/bin/gitlab.pl --url https://gitlab.example.com --token-env STATUS_TOKEN --project tidewater/model \
     export exports --since 2026-10-05
perl $KIT/bin/status-metrics.pl collect --config metrics.json --history exports/history.jsonl</code></pre>
<table>
<tr><th>File</th><th>Columns</th><th>Feeds</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>ci_jobs.csv</code></td><td>ts, job, branch, result, minutes</td><td>pipeline health; time to restore a red <code>develop</code> (W); docs regeneration time (K)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>mrs.csv</code></td><td>iid, title, author, source, target, created_at, merged_at, lead_hours, labels, state</td><td>MR lead time (N), who merges (Z)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>issues.csv</code></td><td>iid, type, title, labels, milestone, assignee, state, created_at, closed_at</td><td>sprint content, carryover, DR closure</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>defects.csv</code></td><td>id, type, phase, found, closed</td><td>interface mismatches caught before integration (H)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tracker/sprint-N.csv</code></td><td>the stories of sprint N with status, blocked_days, blocker</td><td>sprint completion (X), blocked days and top blocker (Y)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The token needs only <code>read_api</code>. Make it a project access token, store it as a masked, protected CI/CD
variable, and give it an expiry date (see below).</p>

<h2 id="admin">Administration</h2>
<div class="why"><b>The objection:</b> "IT runs the servers." <b>Why bother:</b> IT runs GitLab; the project's own upkeep (who has access, which tokens exist, whether the runner works) is configuration management, and it is a few minutes a week when kept up and a crisis when not.</div>
<p>Tom (configuration management) and Jim (owner) keep the project running. The wiki page <em>Administration</em> is the
checklist; here is the reasoning.</p>
<h3>Runners</h3>
<p>A runner executes the jobs. The pipeline needs Perl, Git and a POSIX shell, and nothing else, so the simplest runner
is a <strong>Windows shell runner using Git Bash</strong>: the same Perl the team uses on their laptops, so "passes on my
machine" and "passes in CI" mean the same thing.</p>
<ol>
  <li>On the runner machine: install Git for Windows; clone the kit (for example to <code>C:\agile</code>); download
      <code>gitlab-runner.exe</code> (the approved build) to <code>C:\GitLab-Runner</code>.</li>
  <li>In GitLab: Settings &gt; CI/CD &gt; Runners &gt; New project runner, tags <code>windows, perl</code>. Copy the runner
      authentication token (it starts <code>glrt-</code>); it is shown once.</li>
  <li>Register and install it as a service, from an administrator prompt:
<pre><code>cd C:\GitLab-Runner
.\gitlab-runner.exe register --non-interactive --url https://gitlab.example.com --token %RUNNER_TOKEN% ^
    --executor shell --shell bash --description tidewater-win
.\gitlab-runner.exe install
.\gitlab-runner.exe start</code></pre></li>
  <li>In <code>config.toml</code>, under the runner, add <code>environment = ["KIT=/c/agile"]</code>, or set
      <code>KIT</code> as a CI/CD variable (the project file does).</li>
  <li>Keep the kit current on the runner: <code>git -C C:\agile pull</code> weekly, or pin it to a tag and update the pin
      by MR.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Tokens</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Use <strong>project access tokens</strong> for automation (the weekly export), never a person's token: they stay
      valid when people leave, and they show up as a bot in the audit log.</li>
  <li>Give each token the smallest scope (<code>read_api</code> for exports, <code>api</code> only for <code>setup</code>)
      and an expiry date. Rotate before expiry: Settings &gt; Access tokens &gt; Rotate, then update the CI/CD variable.</li>
  <li>Store tokens in masked, protected CI/CD variables or in an environment variable on your machine
      (<code>--token-env</code>). Never on the command line (shell history) and never in a file in the repo: the
      <code>secrets</code> job and GitLab's secret detection look for <code>glpat-</code> tokens.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Backups</h3>
<ul>
  <li>The instance: the GitLab administrator's daily <code>gitlab-backup create</code>, plus the configuration and secrets
      files kept apart from it.</li>
  <li>The project, at each baseline: Settings &gt; General &gt; Advanced &gt; Export project (repository, wiki, issues,
      MRs, labels, milestones), stored with the baseline's release evidence.</li>
  <li>The repository and the wiki are Git: a <code>git clone --mirror</code> of each, kept on approved storage, is a
      complete copy of the model's history.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Members: on- and offboarding</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Add people at the lowest role that lets them work: developer for engineers, maintainer only for those who cut
      baselines and manage the vendor branch. Set an access expiration date for temporary members.</li>
  <li>On departure, the same day: remove the member from the group and project, revoke their tokens, rotate any shared
      variable they could read, reassign their issues and MRs, and update <code>CODEOWNERS</code>.</li>
  <li>Review the member list each quarter (Manage &gt; Members, sorted by last activity).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Audit and upgrades</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Audit events (Secure &gt; Audit events, Premium) record changes to protections, members, tokens and approvals.
      Review them monthly and after every change to <code>project.conf</code>.</li>
  <li>Before a GitLab upgrade, read the release notes for removed CI keywords and API changes, then run
      <code>gitlab.pl ci --list</code> and <code>gitlab.pl --dry-run setup</code> against the new version.</li>
  <li>Compare the server with the project file monthly: <code>gitlab.pl --dry-run setup project.conf</code> prints only
      what differs.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="trouble">Troubleshooting</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>remote: GitLab: You are not allowed to push code to protected branches</code></td><td>A direct push to <code>develop</code> or <code>main</code></td><td>Push a feature branch and open an MR (<code>flow start feature</code>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>The first push to a new project is refused</td><td><code>main</code> was protected before it existed</td><td>Push the skeleton first, then <code>setup</code> (<a href="#setup">the order</a>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Two pipelines for every push to an MR</td><td>No <code>workflow:rules</code></td><td>Keep the <code>workflow</code> block of the template</td></tr>
<tr><td>Every job fails with <code>KIT is not set</code></td><td>The CI/CD variable is missing or protected and the branch is not</td><td>Set <code>KIT</code> unprotected, or on the runner's <code>environment</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>lint is not 0/0</code></td><td>A name does not resolve, or a warning was left</td><td>Run <code>model.pl lint</code> locally; the line numbers are Vim quickfix format</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>docs/ is stale</code></td><td>The model changed and the docs were not regenerated</td><td><code>model.pl docs</code>, commit <code>docs/</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>The MR cannot merge: "code owner approval required"</td><td>A path's owner has not approved</td><td>Ask the owner shown on the file; changing <code>CODEOWNERS</code> in the same MR does not help (it is read from the target)</td></tr>
<tr><td>The issue did not close when the MR merged</td><td>The MR merged into a branch that is not the default</td><td>Make <code>develop</code> the default branch, or close it with <code>/close</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>A drop merge conflicts in many files</td><td>Someone edited <code>vendor/prime</code>, or reformatted converted files on <code>develop</code></td><td>Never edit the vendor branch; edit converted files only where a TODO or tag requires it</td></tr>
<tr><td>The <code>baseline</code> job fails on a tag</td><td>Docs not regenerated before the release merge, or a different kit version</td><td>Fix on a <code>hotfix/</code> branch and tag <code>baseline-N.1</code>; never move the tag</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>gitlab.pl</code>: <code>401 Unauthorized</code></td><td>The token expired, or lacks the scope</td><td>Rotate it; <code>api</code> for <code>setup</code>, <code>read_api</code> for <code>export</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Labels like <code>workflow::ready</code> do not replace each other</td><td>Scoped labels are Premium</td><td>On Free remove the old label yourself (<code>/unlabel</code>), or move the card on the board</td></tr>
</table>

<h2 id="glossary">Glossary</h2>
<p>The same glossary is the wiki page <em>Glossary</em> (<code>tools/gitlab/templates/wiki/Glossary.md</code>), so the
team has it where they work.</p>
<!--#markdown templates/wiki/Glossary.md-->

<h2 id="cli">Command reference</h2>
<p><code>bin/gitlab.pl</code> talks to a GitLab server through its REST API (with <code>curl</code>, which Git for Windows
ships) or to the offline emulator, with the same commands. <code>perl bin/gitlab.pl help COMMAND</code> prints the
details.</p>
<pre><code>gitlab.pl [--url URL [--token-env VAR] | --local DIR] [--project PATH] [--as USER] [--now ISO] [--dry-run] COMMAND
  setup FILE                         apply a project file (settings, members, protections, labels, milestones, rules, variables)
  sprints START COUNT [--weeks 2] [--prefix "Sprint "]
  issue new TITLE [--template F] [--label L]... [--milestone M] [--assign U] [--type task|issue|incident]
  issue list [--state opened|closed|all] [--label L] | show IID | close IID | note IID TEXT|--file F | link IID TARGET
  mr new --source B [--target B] --title T [--template F] [--label L]... | list | show IID | approve IID | merge IID | note IID TEXT
  pipeline run REF [--source push|web|schedule] | list | show ID
  tag NAME REF [--message M] | release TAG [--notes FILE] [--name N]
  wiki put TITLE FILE | wiki list
  export DIR [--since DATE] [--until DATE]
  ci [--file .gitlab-ci.yml] [--event push|merge_request_event|tag|schedule|web] [--branch B] [--target B]
     [--tag T] [--var K=V]... [--job NAME] [--manual] [--list]
  flow init | flow start feature|release|hotfix NAME | flow publish | flow finish [--title T] [--template F]
     | flow vendor DROPDIR VERSION | flow sync
  help [COMMAND]</code></pre>

<p style="margin-top:3em;font-size:9.5pt;color:#555">Generated from <code>tools/gitlab/guide.html.in</code> by
<code>perl tools/gitlab/mkguide.pl</code>, which embeds the templates and samples. Tidewater, Northwind Marine Systems
and every name and value here are fictional.</p>

